***REMOVED***"# Microsoft Azure OpenAI On Your Data with Elasticsearch\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"In this notebook we'll use Elasticsearch indices to provide grounding data for queries to Azure OpenAI models using the Azure OpenAI On Your Data service.\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"The Azure OpenAI On Your Data service currently supports three search scenarios for retrieval of documents that will be sent to the LLM for processing:\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"1) full text search\n",
***REMOVED***"2) vector search using Elasticsearch Machine Learning models\n",
***REMOVED***"3) vector search using embeddings generated using Azure OpenAI (Ada).\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"Each of these examples will be covered in the following sections."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c26cd732",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
***REMOVED***"## Requirements\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"For this example, you will need:\n",
***REMOVED***"* Python 3.6 or later\n",
***REMOVED***"* An Elastic deployment meeting the following criteria: with machine learning node\n",
***REMOVED***"***REMOVED**** API version 8.x\n",
***REMOVED***"***REMOVED**** A machine learning node for following the example for vector search using an Elasticsearch text embedding model\n",
***REMOVED***"* An Azure OpenAI Resource\n",
***REMOVED***"***REMOVED**** At minimum, one chat model should be deployed for your resource to enable chatting about your data.\n",
***REMOVED***"***REMOVED**** Optionally, if you would like to try out vector search using the Azure OpenAI Ada model, you will also need to deploy an Ada model to your resource.  The examples below will assume you are using the model `text-embedding-ada-002`, but can be updated to suit your needs.\n",
***REMOVED***"* The [Elastic Python client](https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/client/python-api/current/installation.html)\n",
***REMOVED***"* The [OpenAI Python Client](https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/introduction?lang=python)\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"### Create Elastic Deployment\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"If you don't have an Elastic deployment, you can read more about how to get started here in the official [\"Getting Started\" guide for Elastic Cloud](https://www.elastic.co/getting-started).\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"### Configure Azure OpenAI Resource\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"If you don't have an Azure OpenAI resource, detailed information about how to obtain one can be found in the [official documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/openai/use-your-data-quickstart?tabs=command-line&pivots=programming-language-python) for the Azure OpenAI On Your Data service."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cfff496a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
***REMOVED***"## Install packages and initialize environment\n",
***REMOVED***"\n",
***REMOVED***"The first step is to use `pip` to install all of the packages we need to connect to Elasticsearch and Azure OpenAI services."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "65760c02",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
